Yes there are certainly more belief statments to be made.  Some of my
favorites include:

I (irrationally) hold that there are gods, even though I know that
this is wrong.
I (irrationally) hold that there are no gods, even though I know that
this is wrong.
It is not possible to know whether there are gods, but I believe there
are gods.
It is not possible to know whether there are gods, but I believe there
are no gods.
It is not possible to know whether there are gods, but I am
(irrationally) certain there are gods.
It is not possible to know whether there are gods, but I am
(irrationally) certain there are no gods.
The truth of "gods exist" depends upon situation. (With or without
certainty, and with any combination of knowability and belief across
situations.)
I hold that there are gods (certain or uncertain), yet I also hold
that you may correctly hold that there are no gods.
I hold that there are no gods (certain or uncertain), yet I also hold
that you may correctly hold that there are gods.
I don't care whether there are gods.

And of course we can divide them into categories as well, but they
probably aren't necessary to illustrate theism, atheism, or
agnosticism.

