Yes, this really does all depend on the definition of "god". 
Personally, I use at least two different ones.  I am theistic.  I
define a god to be any entity capable of percieving.  I have a fairly
complicated reason for defining god that way, but I am not going bore
you with it.  However, I also consider a valid usage of god to be what
anybody else would call a god.  In any such case I claim it depends
upon the context.

By no means am I an authority on this, but it seems to me there are a
few conditions that have qualified entities to godhood in the eyes of
various people.  Anything omnipotent or omniscient will do.  Anything
that created or helped create the universe(s) might be a god. 
Anything eternal might be a god.  Any ideal concept (Platonic form)
might be a god.  Anything with the power to destroy a given person
might be held by that person to be a god (such as Emporers).

